WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.346
Welcome to Module One, Lesson Three: the four single-agent patterns.

2
00:00:04.676 --> 00:00:08.359
For each, learn the mechanism, the giveaway keyword, and when it wins.

3
00:00:09.159 --> 00:00:12.885
Picture Priya, who leads the AI team at a video streaming service.

4
00:00:13.215 --> 00:00:16.835
Four requests arrive one Monday, and no single pattern fits them all.

5
00:00:17.635 --> 00:00:20.785
Request one: why did buffering complaints spike last night?

6
00:00:21.115 --> 00:00:24.009
Nobody knows the path in advance, so this is ReAct.

7
00:00:24.809 --> 00:00:27.452
ReAct alternates a thought with a tool action,

8
00:00:27.502 --> 00:00:30.263
choosing each step after seeing the last result.

9
00:00:30.593 --> 00:00:35.065
It adapts and recovers well, but it's sequential, with one model call per step.

10
00:00:35.865 --> 00:00:37.911
Request two is a compliance report,

11
00:00:37.961 --> 00:00:41.085
and legal must approve the plan before anything runs.

12
00:00:41.415 --> 00:00:43.327
That's a job for Plan-and-Execute.

13
00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:46.617
A strong planner writes every step up front,

14
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:49.965
and a cheaper executor runs independent steps in parallel.

15
00:00:50.295 --> 00:00:53.787
A replanner steps in only when a step fails or surprises it.

16
00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:58.910
Request three needs six lookups for every ticket, and the token bill is huge.

17
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:02.241
That points to ReWOO: reasoning without observation.

18
00:01:03.041 --> 00:01:05.385
The planner writes every tool call up front,

19
00:01:05.435 --> 00:01:07.833
with placeholders for results it hasn't seen.

20
00:01:08.163 --> 00:01:10.607
A worker runs them with no model involved,

21
00:01:10.657 --> 00:01:13.873
and a solver answers, in about three model calls total.

22
00:01:14.673 --> 00:01:18.762
Request four is a migration script that must compile and pass its tests.

23
00:01:19.092 --> 00:01:22.649
That's Reflection: a critic checks the draft against a rubric,

24
00:01:22.699 --> 00:01:24.162
and the generator revises.

25
00:01:24.962 --> 00:01:27.364
Its cousin Reflexion, spelled with an x,

26
00:01:27.414 --> 00:01:30.736
saves a self-critique to memory after a failed attempt.

27
00:01:31.066 --> 00:01:33.043
So the lesson carries across attempts.

28
00:01:33.843 --> 00:01:35.073
Here's the keyword map.

29
00:01:35.403 --> 00:01:37.537
Adapting to findings means ReAct,

30
00:01:37.587 --> 00:01:43.097
an approvable or parallel plan means Plan-and-Execute, and fewer tokens means ReWOO.

31
00:01:43.897 --> 00:01:46.357
Real systems compose them: plan first,

32
00:01:46.407 --> 00:01:49.991
run each step as a small ReAct loop, then reflect once.

33
00:01:50.321 --> 00:01:55.071
On the exam, a sensible composition usually beats one pattern forced to fit.

34
00:01:55.871 --> 00:01:59.409
Watch the trap: Reflexion stores critiques across trials,

35
00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:02.242
while self-consistency takes a majority vote.

36
00:02:02.572 --> 00:02:05.427
Chain-of-Verification checks a draft with questions,

37
00:02:05.477 --> 00:02:07.941
and Tree-of-Thoughts branches and backtracks.

38
00:02:08.741 --> 00:02:11.720
Match the phrase in the question, not the fanciest name.

39
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:14.155
Read on for the full comparison table.
